1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,490
Picture yourself walking into a phone shop on a Saturday afternoon.

2
00:00:02,490 --> 00:00:04,710
The phone you want sits behind glass with a twelve-million-dong tag.

3
00:00:04,710 --> 00:00:08,890
Next to it stands a bright red sign promising zero-percent installment financing over twelve months at one million dong a month.

4
00:00:08,890 --> 00:00:12,350
You hand over your ID, tap sign on a tablet, and walk out fifteen minutes later with a sealed phone box...

5
00:00:12,350 --> 00:00:13,920
without spending a single dong from your wallet.

6
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:14,670
Sounds painless, right?

7
00:00:14,670 --> 00:00:19,150
Except stop for two seconds and ask one stubborn question: who lends out twelve million dong for an entire year and expects nothing back?

8
00:00:19,150 --> 00:00:20,390
Nobody in business does that for free.

9
00:00:20,390 --> 00:00:22,740
So if your contract really says zero percent, where did the interest go?

10
00:00:22,740 --> 00:00:27,610
Over the next eight minutes, we trace every message on the tablet, every line in the store's Saturday-night ledger, and Monday morning's bank wire...

11
00:00:27,610 --> 00:00:29,900
to find the six hundred thousand dong hiding right behind the counter.

12
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:35,370
It starts right at the glass display counter in our twelve-million-dong example.

13
00:00:35,370 --> 00:00:40,080
The clerk snaps your ID card, types your income details into a tablet, and taps submit.

14
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:43,280
Watch closely what just happened — and what did not happen.

15
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:46,100
That tap sent an encrypted file across the internet.

16
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:49,780
Zero cash left any bank vault, and nothing hit the store's register.

17
00:00:49,780 --> 00:00:51,950
Now look at where that message traveled.

18
00:00:51,950 --> 00:00:54,600
It did not go to the electronics shop's own bank.

19
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:58,010
It skipped straight over to a separate third party across town.

20
00:00:58,010 --> 00:01:06,240
Keep that first question parked in your head while we watch this next step: if nobody bills you monthly interest, who pays for twelve months of waiting?

21
00:01:07,340 --> 00:01:09,970
That third party is a consumer finance company.

22
00:01:09,970 --> 00:01:15,900
Their scoring engine checks your credit history and flashes back one word fifteen minutes later: approved.

23
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:19,140
You sign an electronic loan contract right at the counter.

24
00:01:19,140 --> 00:01:24,840
Look at the names on that signature page, because this legal handoff changes everything down the line.

25
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:30,260
You just signed a debt promise directly with the finance company, not with the electronics store.

26
00:01:30,260 --> 00:01:34,400
From this exact second, the lender you owe twelve million dong to is them.

27
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:38,930
The store simply handed over hardware and stepped out of the credit relationship.

28
00:01:38,930 --> 00:01:48,820
If cash gets tight five months from now and you miss a payment, the company assessing late fees, dialing collections, and logging your credit bureau file is the finance company.

29
00:01:49,670 --> 00:01:52,430
You head home with the new phone in your bag.

30
00:01:52,430 --> 00:01:56,230
Later that night, the shopkeeper locks up and checks the till.

31
00:01:56,230 --> 00:01:59,240
There is zero cash from your phone in the drawer.

32
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:04,820
Open the store's corporate banking app, and the balance has not moved a single dong either.

33
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:05,800
Nothing arrived.

34
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:10,460
So where is the money for that twelve-million-dong handset sleeping tonight?

35
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:12,850
It sleeps inside an accounting promise.

36
00:02:12,850 --> 00:02:28,430
Under Circular ninety-nine rules, the store's accountant logs twelve million dong in sales revenue and pairs it with a twelve-million-dong receivable under Account thirteen eighty-eight — Other Receivables, naming the finance company as payer, never you.

37
00:02:28,430 --> 00:02:33,280
Meanwhile, you already have the phone on your desk without spending cash today.

38
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:34,580
That gap is the loan.

39
00:02:35,920 --> 00:02:43,620
Wait until the next business morning — Day T plus one in our example — and real money finally moves across bank rails.

40
00:02:43,620 --> 00:02:49,420
The finance company fires off a bank transfer into the shop's corporate checking account.

41
00:02:49,420 --> 00:02:52,680
Now look at the credit alert on the owner's phone.

42
00:02:52,680 --> 00:02:56,270
The incoming deposit does not read twelve million dong.

43
00:02:56,270 --> 00:02:59,600
It reads eleven million four hundred thousand dong.

44
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:03,970
Six hundred thousand dong vanished before the wire left the lender.

45
00:03:03,970 --> 00:03:13,300
That five-percent slice withheld at source is the interest subsidy the shop quietly agreed to pay so your paperwork could display zero percent.

46
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:27,450
On the store's books, Account eleven two rises by eleven point four million, Account six forty-one absorbs six hundred thousand as selling expense, and yesterday's Account thirteen eighty-eight promise clears to zero.

47
00:03:27,450 --> 00:03:29,210
Interest was never missing.

48
00:03:29,210 --> 00:03:32,790
The merchant just picked up the tab behind the counter.

49
00:03:34,250 --> 00:03:37,700
Now trace your side of the deal for the rest of the year.

50
00:03:37,700 --> 00:03:48,050
At the end of every month, your checking account sends an instruction to your bank, and one million dong in real money travels across payment rails to the finance company.

51
00:03:48,050 --> 00:03:53,980
Twelve transfers equal twelve million dong flat, matching the counter flyer down to the last dong.

52
00:03:53,980 --> 00:03:57,670
Not a single interest charge shows up on your monthly alerts.

53
00:03:57,670 --> 00:04:00,090
Still, put two real prices side by side.

54
00:04:00,090 --> 00:04:09,170
Suppose paying cash at the counter would have knocked six hundred thousand dong off the bill, dropping the phone's price to eleven point four million.

55
00:04:09,170 --> 00:04:17,770
When you choose installments at twelve million instead, you spend twelve million across a year for gear worth eleven point four million today.

56
00:04:17,770 --> 00:04:22,810
What is left is a simple math question: how steep is that spread as an annual rate?

57
00:04:23,990 --> 00:04:31,270
Careful here: six hundred thousand divided by twelve million is five percent, yet your real borrowing rate is about twice that.

58
00:04:31,270 --> 00:04:31,520
Why?

59
00:04:31,520 --> 00:04:35,820
Because you do not hold all eleven point four million dong for a full year.

60
00:04:35,820 --> 00:04:39,370
Every thirty days, you hand back another million of principal.

61
00:04:39,370 --> 00:04:49,110
Financial analysts match all twelve one-million-dong payments back to the eleven point four million received on day one and solve for the monthly internal rate of return.

62
00:04:49,110 --> 00:04:56,500
In this example, that rate comes out to zero point seven nine eight percent per month — call it zero point eight percent monthly.

63
00:04:56,500 --> 00:05:04,980
Multiply straight by twelve and you get nine point six percent; compound monthly and you hit ten point zero one percent, roughly ten percent a year.

64
00:05:04,980 --> 00:05:15,910
Worse yet, if the contract requires your first one million down at the counter right away, your effective rate jumps to zero point nine five percent a month, or roughly twelve percent a year.

65
00:05:17,580 --> 00:05:24,410
So standing at the register, how do you know whether that six hundred thousand comes out of your pocket or the shopkeeper's?

66
00:05:24,410 --> 00:05:27,050
Ask one plain question: do cash buyers pay less?

67
00:05:27,050 --> 00:05:32,500
Case one: yes, cash buyers save six hundred thousand and walk out paying eleven point four million.

68
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:38,830
Then choosing zero percent at twelve million means you paid the entire interest bill by walking past that discount.

69
00:05:38,830 --> 00:05:42,520
Case two: everybody pays twelve million flat, cash or installments.

70
00:05:42,520 --> 00:05:52,700
Then the shop eats the six hundred thousand out of gross profit — or padded that five percent onto every sticker tag in advance, meaning cash shoppers help subsidize installment buyers.

71
00:05:52,700 --> 00:06:00,240
Why do merchants swallow six hundred thousand when a regular two-point-two-percent card swipe only costs two hundred sixty-four thousand?

72
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:06,730
Because a big zero-percent banner moves phones off the shelf tomorrow morning and puts cash in the bank at T plus one.

73
00:06:07,920 --> 00:06:15,330
There is a second flavor of zero percent you see everywhere: converting a credit card purchase into monthly installments.

74
00:06:15,330 --> 00:06:22,730
Here you swipe the full twelve million dong on your card as usual, then tap convert to twelve months in your banking app.

75
00:06:22,730 --> 00:06:28,730
Now the phone shop takes a normal card payout, and your lender becomes your own card-issuing bank.

76
00:06:28,730 --> 00:06:40,110
Monthly statements still print zero percent interest, yet banks frequently tack on an upfront conversion fee — say three percent, or three hundred sixty thousand dong, billed right away.

77
00:06:40,110 --> 00:06:50,330
Netting that fee against your twelve-million-dong purchase gives an effective rate near zero point four seven percent a month, roughly five point eight percent a year.

78
00:06:50,330 --> 00:06:59,870
Add a forfeited six-hundred-thousand cash discount on top of that conversion fee, and your combined annual borrowing cost pushes close to seventeen percent.

79
00:07:01,170 --> 00:07:08,060
Look at three ways real life knocks this setup off script, because each one reveals who holds your contract.

80
00:07:08,060 --> 00:07:14,440
First, suppose you come into extra cash at month four and pay off the remaining eight million early.

81
00:07:14,440 --> 00:07:30,010
Since your contract prints zero percent interest, there is zero future interest to shave off — and forfeiting a cash discount just packed all six hundred thousand into four months, lifting your real cost to one point four eight percent a month.

82
00:07:30,010 --> 00:07:41,120
Second, miss month five by thirty days with a one-hundred-fifty-thousand late fee, and your real rate climbs near one percent monthly while your credit file logs a late mark.

83
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:51,780
Third, return the phone next week: the shop takes back the hardware, yet only the finance company cancels your loan once the shop wires back eleven point four million.

84
00:07:53,010 --> 00:07:56,430
In the end, the flyer on the glass counter told the strict legal truth.

85
00:07:56,430 --> 00:07:58,980
Your contract really did print zero percent interest.

86
00:07:58,980 --> 00:08:01,820
Money just refuses to work for a year without getting paid.

87
00:08:01,820 --> 00:08:12,230
Instead of living on an interest-rate line, that six hundred thousand dong moved behind the register into the gap between twelve million on the price tag and eleven point four million hitting the shop's bank account.

88
00:08:12,230 --> 00:08:19,700
Next time you spot a bold zero-percent sign, ask the clerk one simple question before you sign anything: what is the price if I pay cash in full right now?

89
00:08:19,700 --> 00:08:22,500
Whatever discount shows up is the real price of borrowing.

90
00:08:22,500 --> 00:08:27,430
From there, you know right away who is paying for the phone's time — the store, cash shoppers, or you.
